Why Are Engineered Substrates Becoming Important in Semiconductor Manufacturing?

Engineered substrates provide the foundation for semiconductor devices that require improved electrical performance, thermal management and manufacturing reliability. Conventional substrate materials can face limitations when devices operate at higher frequencies, voltages and temperatures.

GaN-on-Si technology addresses several of these requirements by enabling gallium nitride device fabrication on silicon wafers. This approach combines the electrical characteristics of GaN with the established manufacturing infrastructure and cost advantages associated with silicon.

QST substrates offer another approach by improving thermal management and mechanical stability. These properties are particularly relevant to high-power applications where heat dissipation and structural reliability directly influence device performance.

Market Growth Is Closely Linked to Energy-Efficient Power Electronics

One of the major factors influencing the Engineered Substrate Market is the increasing demand for energy-efficient electronic systems. Electric vehicles, industrial equipment and renewable energy installations require semiconductor devices capable of handling higher power levels while minimizing energy losses.

SemiconductorInsight identifies growing demand for energy-efficient power electronics as a key market driver. GaN-based devices can support efficient power conversion and switching, making engineered substrates relevant to applications such as EV power modules, fast-charging systems and industrial electronics.

The growing use of advanced RF components in communication infrastructure is also supporting market development. High-frequency applications require substrates that can maintain electrical performance and thermal stability under demanding operating conditions.

