The report also identifies satellite communications, radar systems and defense applications as important areas where improved mechanical stability and electrical isolation can support advanced semiconductor development.

Automotive Electrification Creates New Growth Opportunities

The transition toward electric mobility is another important development influencing the market. EV power electronics require efficient semiconductor devices for power conversion, battery management and charging infrastructure.

GaN-on-Si and QST substrates can support the development of high-performance devices designed for demanding electrical and thermal environments. Increasing investments in electric vehicles, charging networks and renewable energy systems are therefore creating additional opportunities for substrate manufacturers.

The growing deployment of grid-connected inverters and energy conversion equipment also provides opportunities for GaN-based semiconductor technologies.

Manufacturing Challenges and Material Competition Remain Important

Despite strong growth opportunities, engineered substrate manufacturers face several technical challenges.

Yield variability during wafer fabrication remains an important restraint. Producing high-quality compound semiconductor layers on silicon substrates requires careful control of defect density, uniformity and deposition processes. Manufacturing inconsistencies can affect device reliability and increase production costs.

Thermal management complexity and high initial capital expenditure also influence market adoption. Establishing advanced epitaxial growth and substrate-processing infrastructure requires substantial investment.

Competition from alternative materials, particularly silicon carbide (SiC), presents another challenge. Manufacturers must evaluate material performance, production economics and application requirements when selecting substrate technologies.

